# EVERY DECLARED MAPPING, BESIDE WHETHER A LAW ACTUALLY GOVERNS IT. One row per table that names a
# BPMN element, with the law it claims and whether that law exists.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE HOLE THIS CLOSES: A LAW COUNTS AN ELEMENT KIND, AND TWO TABLES CAN NAME ONE KIND.
#    `draw` and `induction` both map to `laneSet`. One lane set is emitted per document, the law
#    counting lane sets passes, and NEITHER table is read by it. So a table declared as mapping
#    and never rendered was invisible to every cardinality law in `diagrams/roster.sqlc`, and
#    `composition_citation` was exactly that: it claims `documentation`, and no citation is
#    emitted anywhere. Counting KINDS cannot verify ATTRIBUTION, and this is the repair.
#
# ⭐⭐ SO GOVERNANCE IS DECLARED RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM THE ELEMENT. Written down, `draw` turns
#    out to be governed by `in_a_lane`, whose model side really is the draws, and not by the law
#    that shares its element name. Inference would have called that a pass.
#
# ⛔ A ROW IS UNGOVERNED WHEN IT NAMES A LAW THAT DOES NOT EXIST, or when it names no law and does
#   not say what it loses. Naming no law is permitted ONLY beside a `loses` that explains it: an
#   element declared, not rendered, and no cardinality law able to see the difference is a fact
#   this repository is willing to hold, and not one it will hold silently.
-- diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc's governance claims against diagrams/roster.sqlc.
SELECT d.object, d.element, d.governed_by, d.loses,
       (d.governed_by IS NOT NULL AND l.slug IS NULL)          AS names_a_law_that_is_not_there,
       (d.governed_by IS NULL AND d.loses IS NULL)             AS ungoverned_and_unexplained
FROM      (
    :compose(diagrams/domain_objects.sqlc)
) d
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(diagrams/roster.sqlc)
) l ON l.slug = d.governed_by
WHERE d.element IS NOT NULL
